WEBVTT
TITLE Recurrence Relations — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
A recurrence relation does not hand you the term. It tells you how to reach it from the terms just before it.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Every recurrence has two halves: base cases that are given outright, and a rule that moves one step forward.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Read the notation in words: a sub n is the term at position n, and a sub n minus one is the term immediately before it.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Two recurrences can share a rule and differ only in their base cases — and then they are different sequences.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Count the n-bit strings with no two consecutive ones. Do not look at the whole string; look at its last bit.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:45.000
If the last bit is zero, the first n minus one bits may be any valid string. That contributes a sub n minus one.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.000
If the last bit is one, the bit before it must be zero. The first n minus two bits are then free, giving a sub n minus two.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:01:00.000
So a sub n equals a sub n minus one plus a sub n minus two, with a sub one equal to two and a sub two equal to three.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Here the rule is a sub n equals a sub n minus one plus six n squared plus two n, with a sub one equal to eight.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Write the rule out for every step and add the equations. The a terms cancel in pairs, leaving a sum.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The two sums are known: the sum of squares and the sum of first powers. Together they give a sub 99 equal to one million nine hundred eighty thousand.

12
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Written as K times ten to the fourth, the value is 198 times ten to the fourth, so K is 198.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
For a linear homogeneous recurrence with constant coefficients, try a solution of the form r to the n.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The example is T of n equals five T of n minus one minus six T of n minus two, which becomes r squared minus five r plus six equals zero.

15
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.000
Two distinct roots give two independent solutions, so the general answer is A times two to the n plus B times three to the n.

16
00:01:53.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Solving gives B equal to zero, so the term is exactly two to the n. The larger root contributes nothing.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Now let the two roots coincide. T of n equals four T of n minus one minus four T of n minus two has the single root two, twice.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
One root gives one free constant, but two base cases need two. The repair is to multiply the second copy by n.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
With a repeated root the solution is A plus B n, all multiplied by r to the n. Here that is one plus n, times two to the n.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
The extra n is not decoration. It is the second independent solution that a repeated root forces you to supply.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
T of n equals two T of n minus one plus n times two to the n, with T of zero equal to one. The forcing shares the base two.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Divide the whole equation by two to the n. Then S of n equals S of n minus one plus n, a plain first-order recurrence.

23
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Unrolling gives S of n as one plus the sum of one to n, which is quadratic. So T of n is Theta of n squared times two to the n.

24
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Three sample values are not evidence. At n equal to three the true value is fifty-six, while four to the n would give sixty-four.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Draw T of n equals two T of n over two plus n as a tree: each node costs n, and has two children of half the size.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Every level adds up to n, because halving the size doubles the number of nodes. The level cost is constant.

27
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:23.000
Halving reaches size one after log base two of n levels, so the total is n times log n, plus the work at the leaves.

28
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:30.000
The tree is a proof sketch, not a proof. It assumes the level cost really is constant and the sizes really do halve exactly.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
For T of n equals a T of n over b plus f of n, compare f of n with the watershed n to the log base b of a.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Forcing smaller than the watershed: the leaves dominate. Equal: multiply by log n. Larger and regular: the top dominates.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:53.000
T of n equals two T of n over two plus n log n sits between the first two cases. The plain theorem is silent.

32
00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:00.000
The extended form handles it: the answer is n times log n squared, over two. Never force a case that does not fit.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Order the standard answers: log n, then n, then n log n, then n squared, then two to the n, then n factorial.

34
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
T of n equals T of n minus one plus one is linear. T of n equals two T of n minus one plus one is exponential.

35
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.000
Base cases change the constant, and sometimes the whole answer: two T of n minus one plus n is n times two to the n.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
A bound is not an equality. Theta is tight, big O is only an upper bound, and GATE usually asks for the tight one.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Checklist: is it first order, linear with constant coefficients, or divide and conquer? Each answer picks the method.

38
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Trap one: assuming the largest root dominates. Trap two: forgetting the n when a root repeats.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Trap three: applying the master theorem inside its gap. Trap four: guessing the answer from three small values.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Then verify: substitute the closed form back into the recurrence, and check it reproduces both base cases.

